 Argentina's 5.288 meaning 5,288) while the TTCI Index column uses commas as decimal separators (e.g., 4,20 meaning 4.20). For the question about maximum tourism income as a percentage of GDP in 2003, I look down the 'Tourism income % GDP 2003' column and find values 1.8, 0.5, 1.9, 8.1, n/d, 18.8, 1.6, 6.3, 1.6, 3.6 — these use dots as decimal separators and the largest is 18.8 for the Dominican Republic, which in the comma-as-decimal convention used elsewhere in the table would be written as 18,8.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Columns like 'Internl. tourism arrivals 2010' use dots as thousands separators (e.g., 5.288, 22.395) while the '2011 TTCI Index' column uses commas as decimal separators (e.g., 4,20, 4,36), mixing regional number formats throughout the table.", "answer": "18,8", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-1384_decimal_separator_swap", "query": "were the highest unemployment rates in japan before or after the year 2000?", "table_markdown": "| Year | Output Growth Rate % | Unemployment Rate % | Inflation Rate % |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1990 | 5,2 | 2,1 | 2,4 |\n| 1991 | 3,4 | 2,1 | 3,0 |\n| 1992 | 1,0 | 2,2 | 1,7 |\n| 1993 | 0,2 | 2,5 | 0,6 |\n| 1994 | 1,1 | 2,9 | 0,1 |\n| 1995 | 1,9 | 3,1 | –0.4 |\n| 1996 | 3,4 | 3,4 | –0.8 |\n| 1997 | 1,9 | 3,4 | 0,4 |\n| 1998 | -1.1 | 3,4 | –0.1 |\n| 1999 | 0,1 | 4,1 | –1.4 |\n| 2000 | 2,8 | 4,7 | –1.6 |\n| 2001 | 0,4 | 5,0 | –1.6 |\n| 2002 | -0.3 | 5,4 | –1.2 |\n| 2003 | 2,7 | 5,3 | –2.5 |\n| 2004 | 3,0 | 5,0 | –1.8 |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/202-csv/28/nt-1384/28_nt-1384_decimal_separator_swap.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "decimal_separator_swap", "reasoning": "Right away the table mixes European-style commas as decimal separators (e.g., Unemployment '2,1', '3,4') with period-based decimals in some negative values (e.g., '–0.4', '-1.1'), but since all values are small single-digit percentages the magnitudes are still clear after mentally normalizing them. To answer the question I scan the Unemployment Rate column: before 2000 the highest value is 4.1% (1999), while after 2000 the values reach 4.7%, 5.0%, 5.4%, 5.3%, and 5.0%, all of which exceed 4.1%. Therefore the highest unemployment rates occurred after the year 2000.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Mixed decimal separators: most cells use commas (e.g., '2,1', '3,4') while some use periods with minus/dash signs (e.g., '-1.1', '–0.4', '–1.6')", "answer": "after", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-1521_column_misalignment", "query": "what is the difference between vladimir gadzhev and yordan miliev's scores?", "table_markdown": "| Player | League | Cup | Europa League | Total |   |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Garra Dembele | 25 | 3 | 8 | 36 |  |\n| Daniel Mladenov | 5 | 0 | 5 | 10 |  |\n| Ismail Isa | 3 | 1 | 4 | 8 |  |\n| Hristo Yovov | 6 | 0 | 1 | 7 |  |\n| Joazinho | 2 | 0 | 2 | 4 |  |\n|  | Vladimir Gadzhev | 2 | 1 | 1 | 4 |\n| Darko Tasevski | 4 | 0 | 0 | 4 |  |\n| Alex | 3 | 0 | 0 | 3 |  |\n| Jeanvion Yulu-Matondo | 3 | 0 | 0 | 3 |  |\n| Mariyan Ognyanov | 2 | 0 | 0 | 2 |  |\n| Serginho Greene | 1 | 0 | 1 | 2 |  |\n| Yordan Miliev | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |  |\n|  | Ivo Ivanov | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |\n| Total | 58 | 5 | 22 | 85 |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/204-csv/784/nt-1521/784_nt-1521_column_misalignment.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "horizontal_shift", "reasoning": "To find Vladimir Gadzhev's total, I look at his row and notice it's shifted right by one column — the Player cell is empty, 'Vladimir Gadzhev' sits under League, and his numeric values (2, 1, 1, 4) land one column to the right of where they should be. Correcting the shift, his total is 4. Yordan Miliev's row is normal with a total of 1, so the difference is 4 − 1 = 3.", "detection_point": "mid_computation", "anomaly_evidence": "Vladimir Gadzhev's row is horizontally shifted: empty Player cell, name under League, and scores shifted right by one column", "answer": 3, "dtype": "int"}
