 1000 inhabitants, while a second row with no country name continues with values like 18 for receipts per capita. After mentally merging these two rows, I scan the 'Tourist arrivals per 1000 inhab (estimated) 2007' column across all countries: Argentina 115, Brazil 26, Chile 151, Costa Rica 442, Cuba 188, Dominican Republic 408, Mexico 201, Panama 330, Peru 65, Uruguay 525. The smallest value is 26, which belongs to Brazil.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Brazil's row is split across two physical rows: the first has arrivals (5,161), receipts (5,919), average receipt (1,147), and arrivals per 1000 inhab (26), while the second continuation row holds the remaining values (18, 3.2, 0.5, 7.0, 52, 4.36).", "answer": "Brazil", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-206_decimal_separator_swap", "query": "what car achieved the highest qual?", "table_markdown": "| Year | Car | Start | Qual | Rank | Finish | Laps | Led | Retired |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1961 | 98 | 5 | 146,080 | 7 | 12 | 192 | 27 | Flagged |\n| 1962 | 98 | 1 | 150,370 | 1 | 7 | 200 | 120 | Running |\n| 1963 | 98 | 1 | 151,153 | 1 | 1 | 200 | 167 | Running |\n| 1964 | 98 | 4 | 155,099 | 4 | 23 | 55 | 7 | Pit fire |\n| 1965 | 98 | 5 | 158,625 | 5 | 2 | 200 | 0 | Running |\n| 1966 | 98 | 4 | 162,484 | 4 | 14 | 87 | 0 | Wheel Bearing |\n| 1967 | 40 | 6 | 166,075 | 6 | 6 | 196 | 171 | Bearing |\n| Totals | Totals | Totals | Totals | Totals | Totals | 1130 | 492 |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/339/nt-206/339_nt-206_decimal_separator_swap.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "decimal_separator_swap", "reasoning": "The Qual column immediately stands out because values like 146,080 and 166,075 use commas where decimal points should be — these are Indianapolis 500 qualifying speeds in mph, so 146.080, 150.370, …, 166.075 are the correct readings. After mentally swapping the commas to periods, I need the highest qualifying speed: scanning the corrected values, 166.075 in 1967 is the largest, and that row corresponds to car 40.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Qual column values like 146,080 and 166,075 use commas as decimal separators instead of periods (e.g., 146.080 mph vs 146,080)", "answer": 40, "dtype": "int"}
