n| 50 | Full Blooded Italians\n(Little Guido (2) and Tony Mamaluke) | 1 | August 26, 2000 | 99 | New York, NY | ECW on TNN#56 | Aired 9-8-00 on TV |\n| 51 | Danny Doring and Roadkill | 1 | December 3, 2000 | 122 | New York, NY | Massacre on 34th Street | Doring and Roadkill's reign was the final one in the title's history. |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Inputs/Dataset/204-csv/23.xlsx", "is_distorted": false, "distortion_type": null, "diversification_type": "original", "reasoning": "Looking at the Event column, Hardcore TV #15 appears in row 5 for The Suicide Blondes' second reign on May 15, 1993. The next row after that is the vacated entry (which has N/A for the event), and the row following that—row 6, featuring The Dark Patriot and Eddie Gilbert—lists the event as Hardcore TV #21. Therefore, the next event after Hardcore TV #15 is Hardcore TV #21.", "detection_point": "none", "anomaly_evidence": "", "answer": "Hardcore TV #21", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-1910_decimal_separator_swap", "query": "is the peak of mount keith above or below the peak of north palisade?", "table_markdown": "| Rank | Mountain Peak | Mountain Range | Elevation | Prominence | Isolation | Location |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Mount Whitney | Sierra Nevada | 14,505 ft\n\n4421 m | 10,080 ft\n\n3072 m | 1,646 mi\n\n2,649 km | 36°34′43″N 118°17′31″W﻿ / ﻿36.5786°N 118.2920°W |\n| 2 | Mount Williamson | Sierra Nevada | 14,379 ft\n\n4383 m | 1,677 ft\n\n511 m | 5.4 mi\n\n8.7 km | 36°39′21″N 118°18′40″W﻿ / ﻿36.6559°N 118.3111°W |\n| 3 | White Mountain Peak | White Mountains | 14,252 ft\n\n4344 m | 7,196 ft\n\n2193 m | 67 mi\n\n109 km | 37°38′03″N 118°15′21″W﻿ / ﻿37.6341°N 118.2557°W |\n| 4 | North Palisade | Sierra Nevada | 14,248 ft\n\n4343 m | 2,894 ft\n\n882 m | 32 mi\n\n52 km | 37°05′39″N 118°30′52″W﻿ / ﻿37.0943°N 118.5145°W |\n| 5 | Mount Shasta | Cascade Range | 14,179 ft\n\n4322 m | 9,832 ft\n\n2997 m | 335 mi\n\n539 km | 41°24′33″N 122°11′42″W﻿ / ﻿41.4092°N 122.1949°W |\n| 6 | Mount Humphreys | Sierra Nevada | 13,992 ft\n\n4265 m | 2,563 ft\n\n781 m | 15 mi\n\n24 km | 37°16′14″N 118°40′23″W﻿ / ﻿37.2705°N 118.6730°W |\n| 7 | Mount Keith | Sierra Nevada | 13,982 ft\n\n4262 m | 1,936 ft\n\n590 m | 3.1 mi\n\n5.0 km | 36°42′00″N 118°20′37″W﻿ / ﻿36.7001°N 118.3436°W |\n| 8 | Mount Darwin | Sierra Nevada | 13,837 ft\n\n4218 m | 1,891 ft\n\n576 m | 7 mi\n\n11 km | 37°10′01″N 118°40′20″W﻿ / ﻿37.1669°N 118.6721°W |\n| 9 | Mount Kaweah | Sierra Nevada | 13,807 ft\n\n4209 m | 2,027 ft\n\n618 m | 11 mi\n\n17 km | 36°31′34″N 118°28′43″W﻿ / ﻿36.5261°N 118.4785°W |\n| 10 | Mount Morgan | Sierra Nevada | 13,758 ft\n\n4193 m | 2,648 ft\n\n807 m | 10 mi\n\n16 km | 37°24′19″N 118°43′58″W﻿ / ﻿37.4053°N 118.7329°W |\n| 11 | Mount Gabb | Sierra Nevada | 13,747 ft\n\n4190 m | 2,601 ft\n\n793 m | 4.3 mi\n\n6.9 km | 37°22′37″N 118°48′09″W﻿ / ﻿37.3769°N 118.8025°W |\n| 12 | Mount Tom | Sierra Nevada | 13,657 ft\n\n4163 m | 1,992 ft\n\n607 m | 4.8 mi\n\n7.7 km | 37°22′34″N 119°10′44″W﻿ / ﻿37.3762°N 119.1789°W |\n| 13 | Mount Dubois | White Mountains | 13,565 ft\n\n4135 m | 2,339 ft\n\n713 m | 10 mi\n\n16 km | 37°47′00″N 118°20′36″W﻿ / ﻿37.7834°N 118.3432°W |\n| 14 | Mount Pinchot | Sierra Nevada | 13,500 ft\n\n4115 m | 2,110 ft\n\n643 m | 4.7 mi\n\n7.6 km | 36°56′50″N 118°24′19″W﻿ / ﻿36.9473°N 118.4054°W |\n| 15 | Red Slate Mountain | Sierra Nevada | 13,162 ft\n\n4012 m | 1,736 ft\n\n529 m | 8 mi\n\n13 km | 37°30′27″N 118°52′09″W﻿ / ﻿37.5075°N 118.8693°W |\n| 16 | Mount Ritter | Sierra Nevada | 13,149 ft\n\n4008 m | 3,990 ft\n\n1216 m | 22 mi\n\n35 km | 37°41′21″N 119°11′59″W﻿ / ﻿37.6891°N 119.1996°W |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/204-csv/25/nt-1910/25_nt-1910_decimal_separator_swap.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "decimal_separator_swap", "reasoning": "To compare Mount Keith and North Palisade, I look at their Elevation values: Mount Keith is listed at 13,982 ft (4262 m) and North Palisade at 14,248 ft (4343 m). Both values are consistent with known California peak elevations and use standard US comma-as-thousands formatting, so no repair is needed for these cells. Since 13,982 ft is less than 14,248 ft, Mount Keith's peak is below that of North Palisade.", "detection_point": "none", "anomaly_evidence": "", "answer": "below", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-2182_decimal_separator_swap", "query": "what is the difference in population between enping and heshan?", "table_markdown": "| # | Name | Hanzi | Hanyu Pinyin | Population (2010 Census) | Area (km²) | Density (/km²) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| City Proper | City Proper | City Proper | City Proper | City Proper | City Proper | City Proper |\n| 1 | Jianghai District | 江海区 | Jiānghǎi Qū | 254.365 | 107 | 2.377,24 |\n| 2 | Pengjiang District | 蓬江区 | Péngjiāng Qū | 719.120 | 325 | 2.212,67 |\n| 3 | Xinhui District | 新会区 | Xīnhuì Qū | 849.155 | 1.260 | 673,93 |\n| Satellite cities | Satellite cities | Satellite cities | Satellite cities | Satellite cities | Satellite cities | Satellite cities |\n| 4 | Enping | 恩平市 | Ēnpíng Shì | 492.814 | 1.698 | 290,23 |\n| 5 | Taishan | 台山市 | Táishān Shì | 941.087 | 3.286 | 286,39 |\n| 6 | Kaiping | 开平市 | Kāipíng Shì | 697.395 | 1.659 | 420,37 |\n| 7 | Heshan | 鹤山市 | Hèshān Shì | 494.935 | 1.108 | 446,69 |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/222/nt-2182/222_nt-2182_decimal_separator_swap.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "decimal_separator_swap", "reasoning": "Right away the Population column uses dots as thousands separators (e.g., Enping shows 492.814 and Heshan shows 494.935), which is a European-style formatting swap — these should be read as 492,814 and 494,935 respectively. Computing the difference: 494,935 − 492,814 = 2,121. So the difference in population between Enping and Heshan is 2,121.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Population values like 254.365, 719.120, 492.814 use dots as thousands separators (European format), and Area/Density columns similarly use dots and commas inconsistently (e.g., 1.260, 2.377,24)", "answer": 2121, "dtype": "int"}
{"index": "nt-148_decimal_separator_swap", "query": "who received the least amount of votes?", "table_markdown": "| Riding | Candidate | Gender | Residence | Occupation | Votes | % | Rank | Biographical notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cape Breton—Canso | Mark MacNeill | M | Inverness | Government and Business Policy Advisor | 7.660 | 21,1 | 3rd | Served in Ottawa as a consultant with National Advisory Board on Science and Technology, with the Treasury Board Secretariat and the Solicitor General's Aboriginal Policing Secretariat. |\n| Central Nova | Mary Louise Lorefice | F | Antigonish | Retired educator | 7.659 | 19,6 | 3rd | Lorefice is a retired teacher from Antigonish, who has lived in the community for 38 years. |\n| Cumberland—Colchester—Musquodoboit Valley | Karen Olsson | F | North River | Stay-at-home Mother | 4.874 | 12,3 | 2nd |  |\n| Dartmouth—Cole Harbour | Brad Pye | M | Ottawa, ON | Senior Political Party Program Officer | 12.793 | 31,5 | 2nd | Pye is an international development worker and son of former Dartmouth North MLA Jerry Pye. |\n| Halifax | Megan Leslie | F | Halifax | Community Legal Worker | 19.252 | 42,7 | 1st |  |\n| Halifax West | Tamara Lorincz | F | Halifax | Director of Nova Scotia Environment Network | 12.201 | 29,6 | 2nd | An environmentalist. |\n| Kings—Hants | Carol E. Harris | F | Wolfville | University Professor | 8.291 | 22,0 | 3rd | She was the NDP's candidate in the 2000 election in Esquimalt—Juan de Fuca. |\n| Sackville—Eastern Shore | Peter Stoffer | M | Windsor Junction | Parliamentarian | 24.279 | 61,4 | 1st | Stoffer has been the incumbent MP since 1997. |\n| South Shore—St. Margaret's | Gordon S. Earle | M | Upper Tantallon | Retired Public Servant | 13.456 | 33,7 | 2nd | Earle is the former federal MP for Halifax West (1997–2000). Has run in this riding unsuccessfully in 2004 and 2006. |\n| Sydney—Victoria | Wayne McKay | M | Sydney | Teacher | 8.559 | 24,4 | 2nd |  |\n| West Nova | George Barron | M | Bear River | Paramedic | 7.097 | 16,9 | 3rd | Barron was born near Paris, Ontario, he has been a paramedic for 22 years. He was nominated on October 28, 2007. |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/204-csv/786/nt-148/786_nt-148_decimal_separator_swap.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "decimal_separator_swap", "reasoning": "Right away the Votes column uses dots as thousands separators (e.g., '7.660' meaning 7,660 and '24.279' meaning 24,279) and the % column uses commas as decimal points (e.g., '21,1' meaning 21.1%), which is a European-style decimal/thousands swap. After mentally restoring the correct format, the vote counts range from 4,874 (Karen Olsson in Cumberland—Colchester—Musquodoboit Valley) up to 24,279 (Peter Stoffer), so the candidate who received the least votes is Karen Olsson with 4,874.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Votes column uses periods as thousands separators (e.g., '7.660', '12.793', '24.279') and % column uses commas as decimal separators (e.g., '21,1', '31,5')", "answer": "Karen Olsson", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-2490_column_misalignment", "query": "what nation was next to great britain in total medal count?", "table_markdown": "| Rank | Nation | Gold | Silver | Bronze | Total |   |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | 1 | Great Britain (GBR) | 2 | 1 | 2 | 5 |\n| 2 | Brazil (BRA) | 2 | 0 | 0 | 2 |  |\n|  | 3 | Spain (ESP) | 1 | 2 | 0 | 3 |\n| 4 | Austria (AUT) | 1 | 1 | 0 | 2 |  |\n| 4 | Greece (GRE) | 1 | 1 | 0 | 2 |  |\n| 4 | United States (USA) | 1 | 1 | 0 | 2 |  |\n| 7 | France (FRA) | 1 | 0 | 1 | 2 |  |\n| 7 | Israel (ISR) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |  |\n| 7 | Norway (NOR) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |  |\n| 10 | Ukraine (UKR) | 0 | 2 | 0 | 2 |  |\n| 11 | China (CHN) | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |  |\n| 11 | Czech Republic (CZE) | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |  |\n| 11 | Canada (CAN) | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |  |\n| 14 | Denmark (DEN) | 0 | 0 | 2 | 2 |  |\n| 15 | Argentina (ARG) | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 |  |\n| 15 | Italy (ITA) | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 |  |\n| 15 | Japan (JPN) | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 |  |\n| 15 | Poland (POL) | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 |  |\n| 15 | Slovenia (SLO) | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 |  |\n| 15 | Sweden (SWE) | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 |  |\n| Total |  | 11 | 11 | 11 | 33 |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/175/nt-2490/175_nt-2490_column_misalignment.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "horizontal_shift", "reasoning": "Looking at the table, rows 1 and 3 are clearly shifted right by one column — for instance, the Great Britain row shows Rank blank, Nation='1', Gold='Great Britain (GBR)', Silver=2, Bronze=1, and the numeric medal values are pushed into the wrong columns. Repairing by shifting those rows left, Great Britain's corrected medals are Gold=2, Silver=1, Bronze=2, Total=5, and Spain (row 3) becomes Gold=1, Silver=2, Bronze=0, Total=3. The question asks which nation was next to Great Britain in total medal count: Great Britain has 5 and Spain has 3, making Spain the next highest, so the answer is Spain.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Rows for Rank 1 (Great Britain) and Rank 3 (Spain) have empty Rank cells, with their rank numbers shifted into the Nation column and nation names into the Gold column, e.g., Rank='', Nation='1', Gold='Great Britain (GBR)'", "answer": "Spain", "dtype": "string"}
