Personal_Finance_Transactions_original_1.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "vertical_shift", "reasoning": "Looking at the table, the PaymentMethod column is clearly shifted down by two rows — the first two data rows (T-5000, T-5001) have blank payment methods, the header label 'PaymentMethod' appears as a value in row T-5002, and three orphan payment method values sit below the last real transaction T-5029. Sliding the entire PaymentMethod column up by two rows realigns it so T-5000 gets 'PaymentMethod' (which is actually the header, so effectively T-5000 gets the value from T-5002's current cell, i.e., the true payment methods shift up by 2). After correcting the alignment and summing CashbackINR by payment method — UPI transactions (T-5002→Debit Card→but after shift: rechecking: the actual payment values shift up so each row's true method is two rows below its current position) — computing totals across all rows, UPI yields the highest total cashback, giving the answer UPI.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "The unnamed column 5 (PaymentMethod) has its first two rows blank, a header value 'PaymentMethod' appearing in row T-5002, and three orphan values ('Credit Card', 'Debit Card', 'Credit Card') sitting below the last real data row T-5029.", "answer": "UPI", "dtype": "str"}
{"index": "nt-148", "query": "who received the least amount of votes?", "table_markdown": "| Riding | Candidate | Gender | Residence | Occupation | Votes | % | Rank | Biographical notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cape Breton—Canso | Mark MacNeill | M | Inverness | Government and Business Policy Advisor | 7,660 | 21.1 | 3rd | Served in Ottawa as a consultant with National Advisory Board on Science and Technology, with the Treasury Board Secretariat and the Solicitor General's Aboriginal Policing Secretariat. |\n| Central Nova | Mary Louise Lorefice | F | Antigonish | Retired educator | 7,659 | 19.6 | 3rd | Lorefice is a retired teacher from Antigonish, who has lived in the community for 38 years. |\n| Cumberland—Colchester—Musquodoboit Valley | Karen Olsson | F | North River | Stay-at-home Mother | 4,874 | 12.3 | 2nd |  |\n| Dartmouth—Cole Harbour | Brad Pye | M | Ottawa, ON | Senior Political Party Program Officer | 12,793 | 31.5 | 2nd | Pye is an international development worker and son of former Dartmouth North MLA Jerry Pye. |\n| Halifax | Megan Leslie | F | Halifax | Community Legal Worker | 19,252 | 42.7 | 1st |  |\n| Halifax West | Tamara Lorincz | F | Halifax | Director of Nova Scotia Environment Network | 12,201 | 29.6 | 2nd | An environmentalist. |\n| Kings—Hants | Carol E. Harris | F | Wolfville | University Professor | 8,291 | 22 | 3rd | She was the NDP's candidate in the 2000 election in Esquimalt—Juan de Fuca. |\n| Sackville—Eastern Shore | Peter Stoffer | M | Windsor Junction | Parliamentarian | 24,279 | 61.4 | 1st | Stoffer has been the incumbent MP since 1997. |\n| South Shore—St. Margaret's | Gordon S. Earle | M | Upper Tantallon | Retired Public Servant | 13,456 | 33.7 | 2nd | Earle is the former federal MP for Halifax West (1997–2000). Has run in this riding unsuccessfully in 2004 and 2006. |\n| Sydney—Victoria | Wayne McKay | M | Sydney | Teacher | 8,559 | 24.4 | 2nd |  |\n| West Nova | George Barron | M | Bear River | Paramedic | 7,097 | 16.9 | 3rd | Barron was born near Paris, Ontario, he has been a paramedic for 22 years. He was nominated on October 28, 2007. |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Inputs/Dataset/204-csv/786.xlsx", "is_distorted": false, "distortion_type": null, "diversification_type": "original", "reasoning": "Looking at the Votes column for all candidates, the values range from a high of 24,279 (Peter Stoffer) down to the lowest. Comparing all entries, Karen Olsson in Cumberland—Colchester—Musquodoboit Valley received 4,874 votes, which is less than every other candidate including George Barron's 7,097 and Mark MacNeill's 7,660. Therefore, Karen Olsson received the least amount of votes.", "detection_point": "none", "anomaly_evidence": "", "answer": "Karen Olsson", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "points_table_2_vertical_shift", "query": "Which country won the most bronze medals without winning any gold medal?", "table_markdown": "| Rank |  |  | Silver | Bronze | Total |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 |  |  | 8 | 0 | 28 |\n| 2 |  |  | 15 | 3 | 29 |\n| 3 | Nation | Gold | 2 | 6 | 9 |\n| 4 | Netherlands | 20 | 1 | 13 | 15 |\n| 5 | Italy | 11 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n| 6 | Belgium | 1 | 1 | 7 | 8 |\n| 7 | Spain | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 7 | Great Britain | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 9 | Germany | 0 | 0 | 2 | 2 |\n| 10 | Greece | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 |\n|  | Russia | 0 |  |  |  |\n|  | Sweden | 0 |  |  |  |\n|  | France | 0 |  |  |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05202026_vertical_shift/points_table_2_vertical_shift.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "vertical_shift", "reasoning": "Looking at this table, the 'Nation' and 'Gold' columns are clearly shifted down — the headers 'Nation' and 'Gold' appear as data in row 3, and the first two data rows have blank Nation/Gold cells, while orphan rows at the bottom (Russia, Sweden, France) have values but no Rank or complete medal counts. Realigning those two columns upward by two rows, the actual data becomes: rank 1 = Netherlands (20 gold), rank 2 = Italy (11 gold), etc., and importantly Germany has 0 gold and 2 bronze, Greece has 0 gold and 1 bronze, and Great Britain has 0 gold and 0 bronze. Among countries with zero gold medals, Germany's 2 bronze is the highest, so the answer is Germany.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Row 3 contains 'Nation' and 'Gold' as data values instead of actual country/number data; the first two rows lack Nation and Gold values; header row is missing 'Nation' and 'Gold' columns.", "answer": "Germany", "dtype": "str"}
{"index": "nt-1733", "query": "how many athletes had a better result than tatyana bocharova?", "table_markdown": "| Rank | Name | Nationality | Result | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Huang Qiuyan | China | 14.39 | SB |\n|  | Anastasiya Juravleva | Uzbekistan | 14.21 |  |\n|  | Zhang Hao | China | 13.63 |  |\n| 4 | Wacharee Ritthiwat | Thailand | 13.54 | SB |\n| 5 | Tatyana Bocharova | Kazakhstan | 13.50 |  |\n| 6 | Wang Kuo-Huei | Chinese Taipei | 13.25 | SB |\n| 7 | Fumiyo Yoshida | Japan | 13.09 |  |\n| 8 | Ngew Sin Mei | Malaysia | 12.93 |  |\n| 9 | Manisha Dey | India | 12.83 |  |\n| 10 | Thitima Muangjan | Thailand | 12.79 |  |\n| 11 | Phan Thi Thu Lan | Vietnam | 12.75 |  |\n| 12 | Lo Yu-Hsin | Chinese Taipei | 12.69 |  |\n|  | Elena Bobrovskaya | Kyrgyzstan | DNS |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Inputs/Dataset/204-csv/910.xlsx", "is_distorted": false, "distortion_type": null, "diversification_type": "original", "reasoning": "Looking at the Result column, Tatyana Bocharova recorded a result of 13.50. Scanning upward for athletes with a higher result: Huang Qiuyan (14.39), Anastasiya Juravleva (14.21), Zhang Hao (13.63), and Wacharee Ritthiwat (13.54) all exceeded 13.50. That gives exactly 4 athletes with a better result than Bocharova.", "detection_point": "none", "anomaly_evidence": "", "answer": 4, "dtype": "int"}
{"index": "nt-241_column_misalignment", "query": "what year has no place indicated?", "table_markdown": "| Season | Tier | Division | Place |   |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1981/82 | 4 | 3ª | 17th |  |\n|  | 1982/83 | 5 | Regional | — |\n| 1983/84 | 4 | 3ª | 1st |  |\n| 1984/85 | 4 | 3ª | 2nd |  |\n| 1985/86 | 4 | 3ª | 1st |  |\n| 1986/87 | 3 | 2ªB | 10th |  |\n| 1987/88 | 3 | 2ªB | 1st |  |\n| 1988/89 | 2 | 2ª | 18th |  |\n| 1989/90 | 3 | 2ªB | 7th |  |\n| 1990/91 | 3 | 2ªB | 8th |  |\n| 1991/92 | 3 | 2ªB | 20th |  |\n| 1992/93 | 4 | 3ª | 14th |  |\n| 1993/94 | 4 | 3ª | 19th |  |\n| 1994/95 | 5 | Reg. Pref. | 9th |  |\n| 1995/96 | 5 | Reg. Pref. | 4th |  |\n| 1996/97 | 5 | Reg. Pref. | 1st |  |\n| 1997/98 | 5 | Reg. Pref. | 2nd |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/231/nt-241/231_nt-241_column_misalignment.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "horizontal_shift", "reasoning": "Looking at the table, the 1982/83 row is clearly shifted one column to the right — the season value '1982/83' sits under Tier, '5' under Division, 'Regional' under Place, and the dash '—' lands in the extra unnamed column, leaving the Season cell empty. Correcting this shift, the row should read Season=1982/83, Tier=5, Division=Regional, Place=—. Since '—' is not a real placement, 1982/83 is the year with no place indicated.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Row for 1982/83 is shifted right: '1982/83' appears under Tier, '5' under Division, 'Regional' under Place, and '—' in the unnamed column, with Season blank", "answer": "1982/83", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "accidents_1", "query": "What is the average of the deaths per accidents per year in the decade 2000s (2000-2009), rounded to 3 decimals?", "table_markdown": "| year | deaths | # of accidents |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 2012 | 794 | 700 |\n| 2011 | 828 | 117 |\n| 2010 | 1115 | 130 |\n| 2009 | 1103 | 122 |\n| 2008 | 884 | 156 |\n| 2007 | 971 | 147 |\n| 2006 | 1294 | 166 |\n| 2005 | 1459 | 185 |\n| 2004 | 771 | 172 |\n| 2003 | 1230 | 199 |\n| 2002 | 1413 | 185 |\n| 2001 | 4140 | 200 |\n| 2000 | 1582 | 189 |\n| 1999 | 1138 | 211 |", "data_file": "dev_test/Diversification/External_Source/workbooks/accidents/accidents.xlsx", "is_distorted": false, "distortion_type": null, "diversification_type": "original", "reasoning": "The decade 2000-2009 covers 10 rows from year 2000 to 2009. For each year I compute deaths/accidents: 2000→1582/189≈8.370, 2001→4140/200=20.700, 2002→1413/185≈7.638, 2003→1230/199≈6.181, 2004→771/172≈4.483, 2005→1459/185≈7.886, 2006→1294/166≈7.795, 2007→971/147≈6.605, 2008→884/156≈5.667, 2009→1103/122≈9.041. Summing these ratios gives approximately 84.366, and dividing by 10 years yields an average of about 8.437.", "detection_point": "none", "anomaly_evidence": "", "answer": 8.437, "dtype": "float"}
