s | 2 | 60 | 0 | 120 | 120 | Standard | 2025-07-16 |  |  |\n| SO-2025013 | 2025-07-14 | C1013 | West | P400 | Coffee Beans | Grocery | 4 | 15 | 0.1 | 60 | 54 | Standard | 2025-07-16 |  |  |\n| SO-2025014 | 2025-07-15 | C1014 | West | P300 | Office Chair | Furniture | 6 | 120 | 0 | 720 | 720 | Express | 2025-07-16 |  |  |\n| SO-2025015 | 2025-07-16 | C1015 | South | P400 | Coffee Beans | Grocery | 2 | 15 | 0.05 | 30 | 28.5 | Express | 2025-07-17 |  |  |\n| SO-2025016 | 2025-07-17 | C1016 | North | P200 | Headphones | Electronics | 4 | 60 | 0.05 | 240 | 228 | Standard | 2025-07-20 |  |  |\n| SO-2025017 | 2025-07-18 | C1017 | South | P200 | Headphones | Electronics | 4 | 60 | 0.05 | 240 | 228 | Express | 2025-07-19 |  |  |\n| SO-2025018 | 2025-07-19 | C1018 | West | P400 | Coffee Beans | Grocery | 6 | 15 | 0.1 | 90 | 81 | Standard | 2025-07-22 |  |  |\n| SO-2025019 | 2025-07-20 | C1019 | West | P400 | Coffee Beans | Grocery | 1 | 15 | 0.05 | 15 | 14.25 | Standard | 2025-07-22 |  |  |\n| SO-2025020 | 2025-07-21 | C1020 | West | P200 | Headphones | Electronics | 7 | 60 | 0 | 420 | 420 | Express | 2025-07-22 |  |  |\n|  | SO-2025021 | 2025-07-22 | C1021 | West | P100 | Laptop | Electronics | 4 | 850 | 0.15 | 3400 | 2890 | Standard | 2025-07-24 |  |\n| SO-2025022 | 2025-07-23 | C1022 | North | P500 | Notebook Pack | Stationery | 7 | 6 | 0 | 42 | 42 | Standard | 2025-07-27 |  |  |\n| SO-2025023 | 2025-07-24 | C1023 | West | P100 | Laptop | Electronics | 1 | 850 | 0.15 | 850 | 722.5 | Express | 2025-07-25 |  |  |\n| SO-2025024 | 2025-07-25 | C1024 | West | P300 | Office Chair | Furniture | 3 | 120 | 0 | 360 | 360 | Standard | 2025-07-29 |  |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Diversification/Self Created Dataset/Manual Created Diversified Dataset/Disturbed Diversifications/Retail_Sales_Orders/Retail_Sales_Orders_original_0.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "horizontal_shift", "reasoning": "Several rows are visibly shifted: SO-2025006, SO-2025007, and SO-2025008 have their data starting two columns to the right (e.g., 'SO-2025006' appears under CustomerID), and SO-2025021 is shifted one column right ('SO-2025021' under OrderDate). After mentally shifting these rows back, I can pair each order's OrderDate with its ShipDate. Counting orders where ShipDate minus OrderDate is ≤ 2 days: SO-2025001 (1d), SO-2025003 (1d), SO-2025004 (1d), SO-2025005 (1d), SO-2025006 (1d), SO-2025009 (1d), SO-2025010 (2d), SO-2025012 (shifted but still within check—actually 3d, no), SO-2025014 (1d), SO-2025015 (1d), SO-2025017 (1d), SO-2025020 (1d), SO-2025021 (2d), SO-2025023 (1d), SO-2025024 (4d, no)—carefully recounting all 24 orders, I find 15 have shipping within 2 days, giving 15/24 = 62.5%.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Rows SO-2025006/007/008 have OrderID in the CustomerID column and SO-2025021 has OrderID in the OrderDate column, indicating horizontal shifts", "answer": 62.5, "dtype": "float"}
{"index": "population_1", "query": "Which region has the highest population density?", "table_markdown": "| Region | Population | Density | Area |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Danish Capital Region | 1823109 | 2,864 km² | 636.5/km² |\n| Remainder | 608036 | 6,970 km² | 87.2/km² |\n| Total for Danish part: | 2431145 | 9,834 km² | 247.2/km² |\n| South Skåne | 683886 | 2,680 km² | 255.1/km² |\n| West Skåne | 305982 | 2,730 km² | 112.1/km² |\n| North-East Skåne | 162829 | 3,705 km² | 43.9/km² |\n| Total for Swedish part: | 1125697 | 11,035 km² | 102.0/km² |\n| Grand Total | 3583842 | 20,869 km² | 171.7/km² |", "data_file": "dev_test/Diversification/External_Source/workbooks/population/disturbed/population_semantic_1.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "label_mismatch", "reasoning": "Right away, the 'Density' column holds values like '2,864 km²' (which are clearly areas) while the 'Area' column holds values like '636.5/km²' (which are clearly densities) — the two headers are swapped. After mentally correcting this label mismatch, the actual density values are in the 'Area' column. Comparing those, Danish Capital Region has 636.5/km², which is the highest density among all listed regions, so the answer is Danish Capital Region.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "The 'Density' column contains area values like '2,864 km²' while the 'Area' column contains density values like '636.5/km²' — the headers are swapped.", "answer": "Danish Capital Region", "dtype": "str"}
{"index": "nt-3_decimal_separator_swap", "query": "how many more passengers flew to los angeles than to saskatoon from manzanillo airport in 2013?", "table_markdown": "| Rank | City | Passengers | Ranking | Airline |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | United States, Los Angeles | 14.749 |  | Alaska Airlines |\n| 2 | United States, Houston | 5.465 |  | United Express |\n| 3 | Canada, Calgary | 3.761 |  | Air Transat, WestJet |\n| 4 | Canada, Saskatoon | 2.282 | 4 |  |\n| 5 | Canada, Vancouver | 2.103 |  | Air Transat |\n| 6 | United States, Phoenix | 1.829 | 1 | US Airways |\n| 7 | Canada, Toronto | 1.202 | 1 | Air Transat, CanJet |\n| 8 | Canada, Edmonton | 110 |  |  |\n| 9 | United States, Oakland | 107 |  |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/515/nt-3/515_nt-3_decimal_separator_swap.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "decimal_separator_swap", "reasoning": "Looking at the Passengers column, values like 14.749 and 5.465 clearly use European-style periods as thousands separators rather than decimals — an airport serving only 14.749 passengers makes no sense, but 14,749 does, and the plain integers 110 and 107 in the last two rows confirm this interpretation. To answer the question, Los Angeles has 14,749 passengers and Saskatoon has 2,282 passengers, so the difference is 14,749 − 2,282 = 12,467.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Passengers column uses periods as thousands separators (e.g., 14.749, 5.465, 3.761) while rows 8 and 9 show plain integers (110, 107), making the format inconsistency obvious", "answer": "12,467", "dtype": "string"}
