{"index": "nt-951_decimal_separator_swap", "query": "how much taller is the mountain peak of mount williamson than that of mount keith?", "table_markdown": "| Rank | Mountain Peak | Mountain Range | Elevation | Prominence | Isolation | Location |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Mount Whitney | Sierra Nevada | 14,505 ft\n\n4421 m | 10,080 ft\n\n3072 m | 1,646 mi\n\n2,649 km | 36°34′43″N 118°17′31″W﻿ / ﻿36.5786°N 118.2920°W |\n| 2 | Mount Williamson | Sierra Nevada | 14,379 ft\n\n4383 m | 1,677 ft\n\n511 m | 5.4 mi\n\n8.7 km | 36°39′21″N 118°18′40″W﻿ / ﻿36.6559°N 118.3111°W |\n| 3 | White Mountain Peak | White Mountains | 14,252 ft\n\n4344 m | 7,196 ft\n\n2193 m | 67 mi\n\n109 km | 37°38′03″N 118°15′21″W﻿ / ﻿37.6341°N 118.2557°W |\n| 4 | North Palisade | Sierra Nevada | 14,248 ft\n\n4343 m | 2,894 ft\n\n882 m | 32 mi\n\n52 km | 37°05′39″N 118°30′52″W﻿ / ﻿37.0943°N 118.5145°W |\n| 5 | Mount Shasta | Cascade Range | 14,179 ft\n\n4322 m | 9,832 ft\n\n2997 m | 335 mi\n\n539 km | 41°24′33″N 122°11′42″W﻿ / ﻿41.4092°N 122.1949°W |\n| 6 | Mount Humphreys | Sierra Nevada | 13,992 ft\n\n4265 m | 2,563 ft\n\n781 m | 15 mi\n\n24 km | 37°16′14″N 118°40′23″W﻿ / ﻿37.2705°N 118.6730°W |\n| 7 | Mount Keith | Sierra Nevada | 13,982 ft\n\n4262 m | 1,936 ft\n\n590 m | 3.1 mi\n\n5.0 km | 36°42′00″N 118°20′37″W﻿ / ﻿36.7001°N 118.3436°W |\n| 8 | Mount Darwin | Sierra Nevada | 13,837 ft\n\n4218 m | 1,891 ft\n\n576 m | 7 mi\n\n11 km | 37°10′01″N 118°40′20″W﻿ / ﻿37.1669°N 118.6721°W |\n| 9 | Mount Kaweah | Sierra Nevada | 13,807 ft\n\n4209 m | 2,027 ft\n\n618 m | 11 mi\n\n17 km | 36°31′34″N 118°28′43″W﻿ / ﻿36.5261°N 118.4785°W |\n| 10 | Mount Morgan | Sierra Nevada | 13,758 ft\n\n4193 m | 2,648 ft\n\n807 m | 10 mi\n\n16 km | 37°24′19″N 118°43′58″W﻿ / ﻿37.4053°N 118.7329°W |\n| 11 | Mount Gabb | Sierra Nevada | 13,747 ft\n\n4190 m | 2,601 ft\n\n793 m | 4.3 mi\n\n6.9 km | 37°22′37″N 118°48′09″W﻿ / ﻿37.3769°N 118.8025°W |\n| 12 | Mount Tom | Sierra Nevada | 13,657 ft\n\n4163 m | 1,992 ft\n\n607 m | 4.8 mi\n\n7.7 km | 37°22′34″N 119°10′44″W﻿ / ﻿37.3762°N 119.1789°W |\n| 13 | Mount Dubois | White Mountains | 13,565 ft\n\n4135 m | 2,339 ft\n\n713 m | 10 mi\n\n16 km | 37°47′00″N 118°20′36″W﻿ / ﻿37.7834°N 118.3432°W |\n| 14 | Mount Pinchot | Sierra Nevada | 13,500 ft\n\n4115 m | 2,110 ft\n\n643 m | 4.7 mi\n\n7.6 km | 36°56′50″N 118°24′19″W﻿ / ﻿36.9473°N 118.4054°W |\n| 15 | Red Slate Mountain | Sierra Nevada | 13,162 ft\n\n4012 m | 1,736 ft\n\n529 m | 8 mi\n\n13 km | 37°30′27″N 118°52′09″W﻿ / ﻿37.5075°N 118.8693°W |\n| 16 | Mount Ritter | Sierra Nevada | 13,149 ft\n\n4008 m | 3,990 ft\n\n1216 m | 22 mi\n\n35 km | 37°41′21″N 119°11′59″W﻿ / ﻿37.6891°N 119.1996°W |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/204-csv/25/nt-951/25_nt-951_decimal_separator_swap.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "decimal_separator_swap", "reasoning": "To find how much taller Mount Williamson is than Mount Keith, I locate their elevations: Mount Williamson (Rank 2) is listed at 14,379 ft and Mount Keith (Rank 7) at 13,982 ft. Subtracting gives 14,379 − 13,982 = 397 ft. Both values look consistent with known California peak elevations and the comma usage is standard US thousands-separator formatting, so no repair is needed for these cells.", "detection_point": "none", "anomaly_evidence": "", "answer": "397 ft", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-1850_broken_rows_split", "query": "who was the last to win against his opponent?", "table_markdown": "| Player | Year | Round | Result | Opponent |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Raymond van Barneveld | 2009 | Quarter-Final | Won | Jelle Klaasen |\n| Raymond van Barneveld | 2010 | 2nd Round | Won | Brendan Dolan |\n| Adrian Lewis | 2011 |  |  |  |\n|  |  | Final | Won | Gary Anderson |\n| Dean Winstanley | 2013 | 2nd Round | Lost | Vincent van der Voort |\n| Michael van Gerwen | 2013 |  |  |  |\n|  |  | Semi Final | Won | James Wade |\n| Terry Jenkins | 2014 | 1st Round | Lost | Per Laursen |\n| Kyle Anderson | 2014 | 1st Round | Lost | Ian White |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/20/nt-1850/20_nt-1850_broken_rows_split.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "broken_rows_split", "reasoning": "Right away I notice that Adrian Lewis (2011) and Michael van Gerwen (2013) each have their data split across two physical rows — the first row carries the Player and Year but leaves Round, Result, and Opponent blank, while the next row fills in those missing fields. Merging these fragments, Adrian Lewis won the 2011 Final against Gary Anderson and Michael van Gerwen won the 2013 Semi Final against James Wade. Looking for the last player (by year) to win, the 2013 winners include Michael van Gerwen (won) and Dean Winstanley (lost), while the 2014 entries both lost, so the last to win against his opponent is Michael van Gerwen.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Adrian Lewis 2011 row has empty Round/Result/Opponent, followed by a continuation row with Final/Won/Gary Anderson; same pattern for Michael van Gerwen 2013", "answer": "Michael van Gerwen", "dtype": "string"}
