across the table: '1' for 'l' and vice versa, 'O' for '0', 'S' for '5', 'B' for '8', 'Z' for '2' in cells like '16,l99,086', '5B,256', '80,522', '93,283', etc.", "answer": "173,805", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-283_broken_rows_merge", "query": "how long has internacional de madrid cf been playing in the 3ª division?", "table_markdown": "| Season | Tier | Division | Place |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 2002/03 | 8 | 3ª Aficio. | 1st |\n| 2003/04 | 6 | 1ª Aficio. | 1st |\n| 2004/05 | 5 | Preferente | 13th |\n| 2005/06 | 5 | Preferente | 4th |\n| 2006/07 | 5 | Preferente | 8th |\n| 2007/08 | 5 | Preferente | 8th |\n| 2008/09 | 5 | Preferente | 3rd |\n| 2009/10 | 5 | Preferente | 2nd |\n| 2010/11 2011/12 | 4 4 | 3ª 3ª | 6th 6th |\n| 2012/13 | 4 | 3ª | 11th |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/783/nt-283/783_nt-283_broken_rows_merge.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "broken_rows_merge", "reasoning": "To count how many seasons the club played in the 3ª division, I look for rows where Division is '3ª'. I find 2012/13 with '3ª' and the row '2010/11 2011/12' whose Division cell reads '3ª 3ª' — this is clearly two seasons merged into one row, so I split it into 2010/11 and 2011/12, each in 3ª. That gives three seasons total in the 3ª division: 2010/11, 2011/12, and 2012/13, so the answer is 3.", "detection_point": "mid_computation", "anomaly_evidence": "Row '2010/11 2011/12 | 4 4 | 3ª 3ª | 6th 6th' contains two merged seasons", "answer": 3, "dtype": "int"}
