DSA | Prof. Krishnan | 39 | 43 | 24 | 11 | 117 | A | True |\n| G-018 | S117 | Spring-2025 | DBMS | Prof. Krishnan | 46 | 49 | 16 | 14 | 125 | A | True |\n| G-019 | S118 | Spring-2025 | DBMS | Prof. Mehta | 32 | 42 | 23 | 13 | 110 | A | True |\n| G-020 | S119 | Spring-2025 | DBMS | Prof. Mehta | 38 | 46 | 21 | 10 | 115 | A | True |\n| G-021 | S120 | Spring-2025 | NLP | Prof. Mehta | 32 | 38 | 24 | 12 | 106 | A | True |\n| G-022 | S121 | Spring-2025 | DSA | Prof. Banerjee | 32 | 36 | 17 | 14 | 99 | A | True |", "data_file": "dev_test/Diversification/Self Created Dataset/Manual Created Diversified Dataset/Disturbed Diversifications/University_Grades/University_Grades_original_2.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "label_mismatch", "reasoning": "Right away I notice there are two columns labeled 'StudentID', and the second one actually contains instructor names like 'Prof. Mehta' and 'Prof. Krishnan' — clearly a label mismatch where that column should be something like 'Instructor'. Setting that aside since the question only asks about student totals, I scan the 'Total(140)' column for the maximum value: the highest is 125 for record G-018, which corresponds to StudentID S117. The answer is therefore {\"StudentID\": \"S117\", \"Marks\": 125, \"Grade\": \"A\"}.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "The second 'StudentID' column contains professor names like 'Prof. Mehta', 'Prof. Krishnan', 'Prof. Banerjee' instead of student identifiers", "answer": {"StudentID": "S117", "Marks": 125.0, "Grade": "A"}, "dtype": "dict"}
{"index": "nt-708_decimal_separator_swap", "query": "what is the total agricultural value in 2008/09?", "table_markdown": "| IME Exchange (Including spot, credit and forward transactions) | 2007/08 | 2008/09 | 2009/10 | 2010/11 | 2011/12 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Agricultural - Volume (thousand tons) | 273,0 | 173,5 | 175,2 | 1.633,9 | 550,0 |\n| Agricultural - Value (billion rials) | 772,6 | 3.729,7 | 484,8 | 3.729,0 | 3.793,0 |\n| Manufacturing and Metal - Volume (thousand tons) | 6.443,8 | 6.679,7 | 7.438,5 | 8.694,0 | 11.685,0 |\n| Manufacturing and Metal - Value (billion rials) | 58.044,2 | 62.120,6 | 53.842,0 | 75.235,5 | 102.356,0 |\n| Oil and Petrochemical - Volume (thousand tons) | 89,7 | 4.339,2 | 7.052,9 | 6.662,6 | 8.117,0 |\n| Oil and Petrochemical - Value (billion rials) | 352,7 | 19.921,0 | 36.450,7 | 41.478,0 | 64.370,0 |\n| Grand Total - Volume (thousand tons) | 6.806,5 | 11.192,4 | 14.666,6 | 16.990,5 | 20.351,0 |\n| Grand Total - Value (billion rials) | 59.169,4 | 82.685,3 | 90.777,5 | 120.443,2 | 170.519,0 |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/204-csv/666/nt-708/666_nt-708_decimal_separator_swap.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "decimal_separator_swap", "reasoning": "Looking at the table, the numbers throughout use European-style formatting where periods serve as thousands separators and commas as decimal separators — for example, '3.729,7' in the Agricultural Value row and '58.044,2' in Manufacturing and Metal Value. Converting these to standard English notation, the Agricultural Value for 2008/09 reads as 3,729.7 billion rials, which is consistent with the magnitude of other values in that row. The total agricultural value in 2008/09 is therefore 3,729.7 billion rials.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Values like '3.729,7' and '58.044,2' use periods as thousands separators and commas as decimal separators (European format), which is a decimal_separator_swap relative to the expected English format.", "answer": "3,729.7", "dtype": "string"}
