{"index": "nt-395_decimal_separator_swap", "query": "what is the pylon with the least height listed here?", "table_markdown": "| Name | Year built | Country | Town | Height | Remarks |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Glacial Aerial Tramway Kaprun III | 1966 | Austria | Kaprun | 113.6 m | The tallest pylon is on the third section |\n| Mississippi Aerial River Transit | 1984 | USA | New Orleans | 109 m | The tallest pylon on a gondola lift; on 87 m pile foundations; demolished 1994 |\n| Torre Jaume I | 1931 | Spain | Barcelona | 107 m | Intermediate stop of the harbour aerial tramway, also observation tower |\n| Gant Hohtaelli aerial tramway |  | Switzerland | Zermatt | 94 m | One pylon |\n| London Emirates Air Line | Early 2012 | UK | London | 88 m | North Main Tower. River Thames aerial crossing between Greenwich Peninsular and Royal Docks |\n| Singapore cable car | 1972 | Singapore | Singapore | 88 m | Pylon I |\n| Eibsee Aerial Tramway | 1962 | Germany | Garmisch-Partenkirchen | 85 m | Pylon II |\n| Nizhny Novgorod Volga Aerial Tramway, Tower 4 & 5 | 2012 | Russia | Nizhny Novgorod | 82 m |  |\n| Mittersill goods aerial tramway | 194? | Austria | Mittersill | 80 m | Two pylons for a tramway that never went in service and was demolished in the 1950s. One of the pylons was built of timber, the other of steel. |\n| Singapore cable car | 1972 | Singapore | Singapore | 80 m | Pylon II |\n| 3S Aerial Tramway | 2004 | Austria | Kitzbühel | 80 m | One pylon |\n| Torre Sant Sebastia | 1931 | Spain | Barcelona | 78 m | Terminal of harbour aerial tramway |\n| Roosevelt Island Tramway | 1976 | USA | New York City | 76 m | Central pylon of commuter tramway |\n| Wendelstein Aerial Tramway | 1970 | Germany | Bayerischzell-Osterhofen | 75 m |  |\n| Vinpearl Cable Car | 2007 | Vietnam | Nha Trang | 75 m | 7 pylons standing in the sea. Total height from sea bed is 115 m |\n| Sandia Peak Tramway | 1965 | USA | Albuquerque | 70.7 m | Pylon 1, inclined at an angle of 18 degrees |\n| Eibsee Aerial Tramway | 1962 | Germany | Garmisch-Partenkirchen | 65 m | Pylon I |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/375/nt-395/375_nt-395_decimal_separator_swap.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "decimal_separator_swap", "reasoning": "Scanning the Height column for the minimum, values range from 113.6 m down to 65 m. The entry '113.6 m' for Glacial Aerial Tramway Kaprun III is the only value with a decimal point, and under a separator swap it could be misread as 1,136 m, but 113.6 m is clearly the plausible real-world height for a pylon, so no repair changes the ordering. The smallest height is 65 m, belonging to the Eibsee Aerial Tramway (Pylon I).", "detection_point": "mid_computation", "anomaly_evidence": "Glacial Aerial Tramway Kaprun III Height '113.6 m' could be read as '1.136 m' or '1,136 m' under separator swap, but 113.6 m is the only plausible reading", "answer": "Eibsee Aerial Tramway", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-1497_vertical_shift", "query": "of the third division, how many were in section3?", "table_markdown": "| Season | Level |  | Section | Administration | Position | Movements |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1999 | Tier 2 |  | South Group | Finnish FA (Suomen Pallolitto) | 9th | Relegation Group - Relegated |\n| 2000 | Tier 3 |  | South Group | Finnish FA (Suomen Pallolitto) | 2nd | Play-Offs - Promoted |\n| 2001 | Tier 2 |  | South Group | Finnish FA (Suomen Pallolitto) | 9th | Relegated |\n| 2002 | Tier 3 | Division | South Group | Finnish FA (Suomen Pallolitto) | 2nd |  |\n| 2003 | Tier 3 | Ykkönen (First Division) | South Group | Finnish FA (Suomen Pallolitto) | 11th | Relegated |\n| 2004 | Tier 4 | Kakkonen (Second Division) | Section 3 | Helsinki & Uusimaa (SPL Helsinki) | 8th |  |\n| 2005 | Tier 4 | Ykkönen (First Division) | Section 3 | Helsinki & Uusimaa (SPL Helsinki) | 6th |  |\n| 2006 | Tier 4 | Kakkonen (Second Division) | Section 2 | Helsinki & Uusimaa (SPL Uusimaa) | 7th |  |\n| 2007 | Tier 4 | Kakkonen (Second Division) | Section 3 | Helsinki & Uusimaa (SPL Uusimaa) | 2nd |  |\n| 2008 | Tier 4 | Kolmonen (Third Division) | Section 3 | Helsinki & Uusimaa (SPL Helsinki) | 2nd |  |\n| 2009 | Tier 4 | Kolmonen (Third Division) | Section 2 | Helsinki & Uusimaa (SPL Helsinki) | 1st | Promoted |\n| 2010 | Tier 3 | Kolmonen (Third Division) | Group A | Finnish FA (Suomen Pallolitto) |  |  |\n|  |  | Kolmonen (Third Division) |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | Kolmonen (Third Division) |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | Kolmonen (Third Division) |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | Kakkonen (Second Division) |  |  |  |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05202026_vertical_shift/nt-1497_vertical_shift.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "vertical_shift", "reasoning": "Right away, the league-name column (third column) is clearly shifted downward — the first few rows are blank or mismatched (e.g., 2005 shows 'Ykkönen (First Division)' but is Tier 4, while orphan values like repeated 'Kolmonen (Third Division)' appear in empty rows at the bottom). Mentally shifting that column up so the league names align properly, the Third Division ('Kolmonen') entries correspond to seasons 2004–2009 (and possibly 2010+). Among those, filtering for 'Section 3' in the Section column gives seasons 2004, 2005, 2008, and 2009 — four rows total, so the answer is 4.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "The unnamed third column has blank/mismatched values in early rows and orphan league names (e.g., 'Kolmonen (Third Division)', 'Kakkonen (Second Division)') sitting in rows with no Season or other data at the bottom, indicating a vertical shift.", "answer": 4, "dtype": "int"}
