{"index": "nt-1042_ocr_char_misinterpret", "query": "nations that had 10 or more medals each", "table_markdown": "| Rank | Nation | Gold | Silver | Bronze | Total |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Cuba | 9 | l1 | 12 | 3Z |\n| Z | Jamaica | 7 | 5 | 4 | l6 |\n| 3 | Co1ombia | 4 | Z | 3 | 9 |\n| 4 | Puerto Rico | 4 | Z | 1 | 7 |\n| S | Mexico | 3 | 3 | 4 | lO |\n| 6 | Trinidad and Tobago | 2 | 5 | 1 | B |\n| 7 | 8arbados | 1 | 1 | 1 | 3 |\n| B | Guatemala | l | 0 | 0 | 1 |\n| 8 | 8ahamas | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |\n| 10 | Venezue1a | 0 | 2 | S | 7 |\n| l1 | U.S. Virgin Is1ands | 0 | 1 | l | 2 |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/466/nt-1042/466_nt-1042_ocr_char_misinterpret.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "ocr_char_misinterpret", "reasoning": "Scanning the table, pervasive OCR noise is immediately apparent: ranks like 'Z' (2), 'S' (5), 'B' (8); nations like '8arbados' (Barbados), 'Co1ombia' (Colombia); and totals like '3Z' (32), 'l6' (16), 'lO' (10), 'B' (8). After repairing these substitutions (l→1, O→0, Z→2, S→5, B→8), I check each nation's corrected Total for ≥10: Cuba has 32, Jamaica has 16, Mexico has 10, and all others fall below 10. The answer is Cuba, Jamaica, and Mexico.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Total column contains 'l6', 'lO', 'B', '3Z' (OCR: l->1, O->0, B->8, Z->2); Nation column has '8arbados', '8ahamas', 'Co1ombia', 'Venezue1a'; Rank column has 'Z', 'S', 'B', 'l1'", "answer": ["Cuba", "Jamaica", "Mexico"], "dtype": "list"}
