{"index": "climate_1", "query": "What is the average of the Average High for the first 6 months of the year in °C?", "table_markdown": "| Month | Jan | Feb | Mar | Apr | May | Jun | Jul | Aug | Sep | Oct | Nov | Dec | Year |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Average high °F (°C) | 17.3\n(63.1) | 19.5\n(67.1) | 22.6\n(72.7) | 25.9\n(78.6) | 27.2\n(81) | 29.3\n(84.7) | 31.8\n(89.2) | 31.4\n(88.5) | 28.9\n(84) | 25.5\n(77.9) | 21.7\n(71.1) | 19.2\n(66.6) | 24.76\n(76.57) |\n| Average low °C (°F) | 7.9\n(46.2) | 9.4\n(48.9) | 12.5\n(54.5) | 17.6\n(63.7) | 19.2\n(66.6) | 21.6\n(70.9) | 23.8\n(74.8) | 22.5\n(72.5) | 20.7\n(69.3) | 16.5\n(61.7) | 14.1\n(57.4) | 8.5\n(47.3) | 15.94\n(60.69) |\n| Precipitation mm (inches) | 235.9\n(9.287) | 129.2\n(5.087) | 82.8\n(3.26) | 33.6\n(1.323) | 4.7\n(0.185) | 0.2\n(0.008) | 0.0\n(0) | 0.2\n(0.008) | 3.2\n(0.126) | 58.0\n(2.283) | 107.4\n(4.228) | 214.5\n(8.445) | 857.3\n(33.752) |\n| Avg. precipitation days | 13.9 | 11.4 | 8.6 | 3.6 | 2.4 | 0.1 | 0 | 0.1 | 1.8 | 4.9 | 8 | 11.8 | 63.7 |", "data_file": "dev_test/Diversification/External_Source/workbooks/climate/disturbed/climate_semantic_1.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "label_mismatch", "reasoning": "Looking at the table, the row labeled 'Average high °F (°C)' has primary values like 17.3, 19.5, 22.6 for Jan–Mar, which are clearly Celsius temperatures (the parenthetical values like 63.1, 67.1, 72.7 are the Fahrenheit equivalents), so the header's unit order is swapped — it should read '°C (°F)'. For the question asking about the average high in °C for the first six months, I take the primary (first) values: 17.3, 19.5, 22.6, 25.9, 27.2, 29.3, which sum to 141.8 and average to 141.8 / 6 = 23.63 °C.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "The 'Average high °F (°C)' row shows primary values like 17.3, 19.5, 22.6 which are clearly °C, not °F; similarly 'Average low °C (°F)' shows primary values like 7.9, 9.4 which are °C with °F in parentheses, contradicting its header format.", "answer": 23.63, "dtype": "float"}
{"index": "nt-1515", "query": "how many works were made in the incidental music genre?", "table_markdown": "| Composer | Title | Genre | Date | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Flor Alpaerts | Salome | incidental music |  |  |\n| Granville Bantock | Dance of the\nSeven Veils | incidental music | 1918 | staged London, 1918 |\n| Leonard Bernstein | Salome | incidental music | 1955 | chamber orchestra with 8 players and vocal soloists; withdrawn |\n| Peter Maxwell Davies | Salome | ballet | 1978 | premiered Copenhagen, 10 November 1978; Danish Radio Concert Orchestra, conducted by János Fürst; scenario and choreography by Flemming Flindt |\n| Pete Doherty | Salome | popular music | 2009 | appears on his album Grace/Wastelands |\n| Alexander Glazunov | Introduction and Dance, Op. 90 | incidental music | 1908 |  |\n| Henry Hadley | Salome, Op. 55 | symphonic poem | 1905 | this was written after Hadley had seen a production of Oscar Wilde's play, and was a favourite among his own compositions |\n| Alexander Krein | Salome, Op. 19 | symphonic poem | 1929 |  |\n| Constant Lambert | Salome | incidental music | 1929 | clarinet, cello, trumpet, percussion; written for a performance staged in Cambridge by Terence Gray, with choreography by Ninette de Valois; staged again November 1931; a suite was arranged by Giles Easterbrook in 1998 and recorded in 2000 |\n| Antoine Mariotte | Salomé | opera | 1905 | premiered 1908; he was involved in a debate with Richard Strauss to prove that his music was written earlier than Strauss's version, also written in 1905 |\n| Emil Petrovics | Salome | ballet | 1978 | flute, trumpet, harp and percussion |\n| Richard Strauss | Salome, Op. 54 | opera | 1905 | trans. Hedwig Lachmann; premiered Dresden 1905. This opera is by far the best known musical adaptation of a work of Oscar Wilde. |\n| Alexander Tcherepnin | Salome | incidental music |  |  |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Inputs/Dataset/204-csv/969.xlsx", "is_distorted": false, "distortion_type": null, "diversification_type": "original", "reasoning": "Looking at the Genre column, I need to count all rows where the genre is \"incidental music.\" The composers with incidental music are Flor Alpaerts, Granville Bantock, Leonard Bernstein, Alexander Glazunov, Constant Lambert, and Alexander Tcherepnin — that gives a total of 6 works in the incidental music genre.", "detection_point": "none", "anomaly_evidence": "", "answer": 6, "dtype": "int"}
{"index": "nt-1564_footnote_injection", "query": "who is listed before keith walker?", "table_markdown": "| Wrestler: | Times: | Date: | Location: | Notes: |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Jonnie Stewart | 1 | June 6, 1996 | Rochester, Minnesota | Defeated Larry Gligorovich to win the AWA Superstars of Wrestling World Heavyweight Championship. |\n| King Kong Bundy | 1 | March 31, 1999 | Oshkosh, Wisconsin | Later stripped of the title by owner Dale Gagne. |\n| The Patriot\n\n(Danny Dominion) | 1 | July 29, 2000 | Pine Bluff, Arkansas | Defeated Dale Gagne in an impromptu match to win the title. |\n| Ricky Enrique | 1 | July 29, 2000 | Pine Bluff, Arkansas |  |\n| Eric Priest | 1 | June 3, 2001 | Hillside, Illinois |  |\n| Evan Karagias | 1 | March 22, 2002 | Casa Grande, Arizona |  |\n| Horshu | 1 | October 12, 2002 | Mercedes, Texas | Stripped of the title due to missing mandatory title defenses. |\n| Evan Karagias | 2 | July 6, 2003 | Lemoore, California | Defeated Eric Priest to win the vacated title. Karagias was fired in January 2005 by Dale Gagne for misconduct and refusal of defending the title as scheduled. |\n| Takao Ōmori | 1 | February 15, 2005 | Tokyo, Japan | Defeated Steve Corino in the finals of a tournament when Pro Wrestling Zero1 joins AWA Superstars of Wrestling. |\n| Steve Corino | 1 | June 11, 2005 | Bay City, Michigan |  |\n| Shinjiro Otani | 1 | January 22, 2006 | Tokyo, Japan |  |\n| Takao Ōmori | 2 | April 1, 2006 | Tokyo, Japan |  |\n| Ric Converse | 1 | June 14, 2006 | Indianapolis, Indiana |  |\n| Steve Corino | 2 | January 7, 2007 | Pottstown, Pennsylvania | On March 23, 2007, Corino lost the title to TNT at an AWF event; however, on March 28, the AWA Superstars of Wrestling Board of Directors returned the title to Corino when the proper paperwork for the match could not be found. |\n| Takao Ōmori | 3 | March 31, 2007 | Yokohama, Japan |  |\n| Masato Tanaka | 1 | October 26, 2007 | Tokyo, Japan |  |\n| Larry Zbyszko | 1* | February 5, 2008 | Minneapolis, Minnesota | Title awarded. |\n| Brian Logan† | # | April 20, 2008 | Fayetteville, West Virginia | Logan defeated Zbyszko and Ricky Landell in a triple threat match. Shortly thereafter, Mountaineer Wrestling Association of West Virginia, the promotion for which Logan primarily performs, pulled out of AWA Superstars of Wrestling to form American Wrestling Affiliates with several other promotions. The title was returned to Zbyszko, and Logan's reign was never officially recognized by AWA Superstars of Wrestling. |\n| Larry Zbyszko | 1* |  |  | Title returned to Zbyszko. |\n| Ricky Landell | 1 | October 11, 2008 | Indianapolis, Indiana | Title becomes known as the WSL World Heavyweight Championship when the promotion is forced to rename. |\n| * See appendix for details. |  |  |  |  |\n| Keith Walker | 1 | February 21, 2009 | Michigan City, Indiana |  |\n| 1) Figures rounded to nearest integer. |  |  |  |  |\n| Jonnie Stewart | 2 | June 9, 2012 | Landover, Maryland | In a day referred to as The Saturday Night Massacre, in reference to President Nixon's firing of two Whitehouse attorneys general in one night; President Dale Gagne strips and fires Keith Walker when Walker refuses to defend the title against Ricky Landell, in an event in Landover, Maryland. When Landell is awarded the title, he refuses to accept and is too promptly fired by Gagne, who than awards the title to Jonnie Stewart. |\n| The Honky Tonk Man | 1 | August 18, 2012 | Rockford, Illinois | The morning of the event, Jonnie Stewart's doctors declare him PUP (physically unable to perform) and WSL officials agree to let Mike Bally sub for Stewart. |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/204-csv/684/nt-1564/684_nt-1564_footnote_injection.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "footnote_injection", "reasoning": "Looking for the wrestler listed before Keith Walker, I scan downward and find Keith Walker's row with a date of February 21, 2009. Just above him, however, there are two injected footnote rows — '* See appendix for details.' and '1) Figures rounded to nearest integer.' — which are clearly not real data entries. Skipping those non-data rows, the actual wrestler row immediately preceding Keith Walker is Ricky Landell, who won the title on October 11, 2008 in Indianapolis, Indiana.", "detection_point": "mid_computation", "anomaly_evidence": "Rows '* See appendix for details.' and '1) Figures rounded to nearest integer.' are footnote rows injected between Ricky Landell and Keith Walker", "answer": "Ricky Landell", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-1540_merge_cells", "query": "how many weeks did season 15 of comedy central presents span?", "table_markdown": "| No. in\n\nSeries |  | Performer | Appearance | Air Date |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 268 | 1 | Tom Segura | only | 21 January 2011 |\n| 269 | 2 | Michael Kosta | only | 21 January 2011 |\n| 270 | 3 | Sheng Wang | only | 28 January 2011 |\n| 271 | 4 | Chelsea Peretti | only | 28 January 2011 |\n| 272 | 5 | Chris D'Elia | only | 4 February 2011 |\n| 273 | 6 | Nate Bargatze | only | 4 February 2011 |\n| 274 | 7 | Hari Kondabolu | only | 11 February 2011 |\n| 275 | 8 | Jack Whitehall | only | 11 February 2011 |\n| 276 | 9 | Al Jackson | only | 18 February 2011 |\n| 277 | 10 | Kyle Kinane | only | 25 February 2011 |\n| 278 | 11 | Matt Fulchiron | only | 25 February 2011 |\n| 279 | 12 | Mike Vecchione | only | 4 March 2011 |\n| 280 | 13 | Jessi Klein | only | 4 March 2011 |\n| 281 | 14 | Louis Katz | only | 11 March 2011 |\n| 282 | 15 | Jay Larson | only | 11 March 2011 |\n| 283 | 16 | Natasha Leggero | only | 25 March 2011 |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/784/nt-1540/784_nt-1540_merge_cells.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "merge_cells", "reasoning": "Right away the header row looks off: the first column header reads \"No. in Series\" but it appears to have been merged with what should be a separate \"No. in Season\" header, leaving the second column blank. Despite this, the data itself is intact — the second column clearly represents the season episode number (1 through 16) and the Air Date column runs from 21 January 2011 to 25 March 2011. Counting the weeks between those two dates: January has the 21st and 28th (2 weeks), February has the 4th, 11th, 18th, and 25th (4 weeks), and March has the 4th, 11th, and 25th (3 weeks), giving 9 distinct weeks total.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "The first header cell reads \"No. in\\n\\nSeries\" — merging what should be two separate headers (\"No. in Series\" and \"No. in Season\") into one cell, leaving the second column unnamed.", "answer": 9, "dtype": "int"}
{"index": "tenure_1", "query": "Who have the shortest tenure?", "table_markdown": "| Name | Term end | Term start |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Pier Ruggero Piccio | 1926-01-01 00:00:00 | 1927-02-06 00:00:00 |\n| Armando Armani | 1927-02-10 00:00:00 | 1928-10-13 00:00:00 |\n| Giuseppe Valle | 1930-02-22 00:00:00 | 1933-11-23 00:00:00 |\n| Antonio Bosio | 1933-11-23 00:00:00 | 1934-03-22 00:00:00 |\n| Giuseppe Valle | 1934-03-22 00:00:00 | 1939-11-10 00:00:00 |\n| Francesco Pricolo | 1939-11-10 00:00:00 | 1941-11-15 00:00:00 |\n| Rino Corso Fougier | 1941-11-15 00:00:00 | 1943-07-27 00:00:00 |\n| Renato Sandalli | 1943-07-27 00:00:00 | 1944-06-18 00:00:00 |\n| Pietro Piacentini | 1944-06-19 00:00:00 | 1944-12-13 00:00:00 |\n| Mario Ajmone Cat | 1944-12-13 00:00:00 | 1951-02-05 00:00:00 |\n| Aldo Urbani | 1951-02-05 00:00:00 | 1955-11-10 00:00:00 |\n| Ferdinando Raffaelli | 1955-11-10 00:00:00 | 1958-02-01 00:00:00 |\n| Silvio Napoli | 1958-02-01 00:00:00 | 1961-09-01 00:00:00 |\n| Aldo Remondino | 1961-09-01 00:00:00 | 1968-02-28 00:00:00 |\n| Duilio S. Fanali | 1968-02-28 00:00:00 | 1971-11-01 00:00:00 |\n| Vincenzo Lucertini | 1971-11-01 00:00:00 | 1974-02-27 00:00:00 |\n| Dino Ciarlo | 1974-02-27 00:00:00 | 1977-06-20 00:00:00 |\n| Alessandro Mettimano | 1977-06-20 00:00:00 | 1980-04-01 00:00:00 |\n| Lamberto Bartolucci | 1980-04-02 00:00:00 | 1983-10-12 00:00:00 |\n| Basilio Cottone | 1983-10-19 00:00:00 | 1986-09-17 00:00:00 |\n| Franco Pisano | 1986-09-18 00:00:00 | 1990-04-15 00:00:00 |\n| Stelio Nardini | 1990-04-16 00:00:00 | 1993-03-24 00:00:00 |\n| Adelchi Pillinini | 1993-03-25 00:00:00 | 1995-06-03 00:00:00 |\n| Mario Arpino | 1995-06-04 00:00:00 | 1999-02-05 00:00:00 |\n| Andrea Fornasiero | 1999-02-05 00:00:00 | 2001-08-05 00:00:00 |\n| Sandro Ferracuti | 2001-08-05 00:00:00 | 2004-08-04 00:00:00 |\n| Leonardo Tricarico | 2004-08-05 00:00:00 | 2006-09-19 00:00:00 |\n| Vincenzo Camporini | 2006-09-19 00:00:00 | 2008-01-30 00:00:00 |\n| Daniele Tei | 2008-01-30 00:00:00 | 2010-02-25 00:00:00 |\n| Giuseppe Bernardis | 2010-02-25 00:00:00 | 2013-02-25 00:00:00 |\n| Pasquale Preziosa | 2013-02-25 00:00:00 | Incumbent |", "data_file": "dev_test/Diversification/External_Source/workbooks/tenure/disturbed/tenure_semantic_1.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "label_mismatch", "reasoning": "Looking at the table, the 'Term end' and 'Term start' columns are clearly swapped — for every row the date under 'Term end' is earlier than the date under 'Term start' (e.g., Pier Ruggero Piccio shows Term end 1926-01-01 and Term start 1927-02-06), which is semantically backwards. After mentally swapping the headers so that the first date column is actually the start and the second is the end, I compute each person's tenure; Antonio Bosio served from 1933-11-23 to 1934-03-22, roughly 4 months, which is the shortest tenure in the list. Therefore the answer is Antonio Bosio.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "The 'Term end' column consistently contains dates earlier than the 'Term start' column (e.g., Pier Ruggero Piccio has Term end 1926-01-01 but Term start 1927-02-06), indicating the labels are swapped.", "answer": "Antonio Bosio", "dtype": "str"}
{"index": "nt-1636", "query": "which country had the highest number of medals?", "table_markdown": "| Rank | Nation | Gold | Silver | Bronze | Total |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Russia | 6 | 3 | 7 | 16 |\n| 2 | United States | 5 | 0 | 4 | 9 |\n| 3 | Japan | 3 | 4 | 1 | 8 |\n| 4 | France | 3 | 1 | 1 | 5 |\n| 5 | Ukraine | 2 | 0 | 2 | 4 |\n| 6 | Turkey | 2 | 0 | 1 | 3 |\n| 7 | Sweden | 2 | 0 | 0 | 2 |\n| 8 | Iran | 1 | 2 | 1 | 4 |\n| 9 | Armenia | 1 | 1 | 2 | 4 |\n| 10 | China | 1 | 1 | 1 | 3 |\n| 11 | Austria | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |\n| 11 | Bulgaria | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |\n| 11 | South Korea | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |\n| 14 | Germany | 0 | 3 | 4 | 7 |\n| 15 | Kazakhstan | 0 | 3 | 0 | 3 |\n| 16 | Cuba | 0 | 2 | 3 | 5 |\n| 17 | Norway | 0 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\n| 18 | Venezuela | 0 | 1 | 1 | 2 |\n| 19 | Canada | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 19 | Hungary | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 19 | Israel | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 19 | Moldova | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 19 | Poland | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 19 | Romania | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| 25 | Uzbekistan | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 |\n| Total | Total | 29 | 29 | 29 | 87 |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Inputs/Dataset/203-csv/812.xlsx", "is_distorted": false, "distortion_type": null, "diversification_type": "original", "reasoning": "Looking at the Total column to find the highest number of medals, Russia leads with 16 total medals (6 Gold, 3 Silver, 7 Bronze), which is well above the next highest, the United States with 9. Therefore, the country with the highest number of medals is Russia.", "detection_point": "none", "anomaly_evidence": "", "answer": "Russia", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-53_broken_rows_split", "query": "what is the total number of pylons listed?", "table_markdown": "| Name | Year built | Country | Town | Height | Remarks |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Glacial Aerial Tramway Kaprun III | 1966 | Austria |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Kaprun | 113.6 m | The tallest pylon is on the third section |\n| Mississippi Aerial River Transit | 1984 | USA |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | New Orleans | 109 m | The tallest pylon on a gondola lift; on 87 m pile foundations; demolished 1994 |\n| Torre Jaume I | 1931 | Spain | Barcelona | 107 m | Intermediate stop of the harbour aerial tramway, also observation tower |\n| Gant Hohtaelli aerial tramway |  | Switzerland |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Zermatt | 94 m | One pylon |\n| London Emirates Air Line | Early 2012 | UK |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | London | 88 m | North Main Tower. River Thames aerial crossing between Greenwich Peninsular and Royal Docks |\n| Singapore cable car | 1972 | Singapore |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Singapore | 88 m | Pylon I |\n| Eibsee Aerial Tramway | 1962 | Germany |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Garmisch-Partenkirchen | 85 m | Pylon II |\n| Nizhny Novgorod Volga Aerial Tramway, Tower 4 & 5 | 2012 | Russia |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Nizhny Novgorod | 82 m |  |\n| Mittersill goods aerial tramway | 194? | Austria |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Mittersill | 80 m | Two pylons for a tramway that never went in service and was demolished in the 1950s. One of the pylons was built of timber, the other of steel. |\n| Singapore cable car | 1972 | Singapore |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Singapore | 80 m | Pylon II |\n| 3S Aerial Tramway | 2004 | Austria |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Kitzbühel | 80 m | One pylon |\n| Torre Sant Sebastia | 1931 | Spain |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Barcelona | 78 m | Terminal of harbour aerial tramway |\n| Roosevelt Island Tramway | 1976 | USA |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | New York City | 76 m | Central pylon of commuter tramway |\n| Wendelstein Aerial Tramway | 1970 | Germany |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Bayerischzell-Osterhofen | 75 m |  |\n| Vinpearl Cable Car | 2007 | Vietnam |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Nha Trang | 75 m | 7 pylons standing in the sea. Total height from sea bed is 115 m |\n| Sandia Peak Tramway | 1965 | USA |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Albuquerque | 70.7 m | Pylon 1, inclined at an angle of 18 degrees |\n| Eibsee Aerial Tramway | 1962 | Germany |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  | Garmisch-Partenkirchen | 65 m | Pylon I |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/203-csv/375/nt-53/375_nt-53_broken_rows_split.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "structural", "diversification_type": "broken_rows_split", "reasoning": "The table clearly has broken rows where each tramway's record is split across two physical lines — for instance, 'Glacial Aerial Tramway Kaprun III' has its Name, Year, and Country on one line but Town, Height, and Remarks on the next. After mentally merging these split rows back together, I count the total number of distinct pylons/entries listed: Kaprun III, Mississippi, Torre Jaume I, Gant Hohtaelli, London Emirates, Singapore (Pylon I), Eibsee (Pylon II), Nizhny Novgorod (Towers 4 & 5 = 2), Mittersill (two pylons), Singapore (Pylon II), 3S Kitzbühel, Torre Sant Sebastia, Roosevelt Island, Wendelstein, Vinpearl (7 pylons), Sandia Peak (Pylon 1), and Eibsee (Pylon I). Tallying the explicit pylon counts from the Remarks — Mittersill has 2, Vinpearl has 7, Nizhny Novgorod lists Towers 4 & 5 so 2, and the remaining entries each reference one pylon — gives 2 + 7 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 17 total pylons.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Rows are split so that Name/Year/Country appear on one physical row and Town/Height/Remarks on the next, e.g., 'Glacial Aerial Tramway Kaprun III' on row 1 with blank Town/Height, then Kaprun/113.6 m on row 2", "answer": 17, "dtype": "int"}
{"index": "nt-1905_ocr_char_misinterpret", "query": "how many votes were counted as invalid or blank votes?", "table_markdown": "| Party | Votes | % | Seats | +/– |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Liberal Democratic League | l64,376 | 28.2 | 34 | +8 |\n| Anti Revo1utionary Party | l43,843 | 24.7 | 15 | –7 |\n| Genera1 League of Roman Catho1ic Caucuses | 76,60S | 13.l | Z5 | O |\n| Social Democratic Workers' Party | 65,56l | ll.Z | 7 | O |\n| Christian Historicals | 62,770 | lO.B | B | –2 |\n| Free-thinking Democratic League | 5l,595 | 8.8 | 11 | +Z |\n| 0ther parties | lB,638 | 3.2 | 0 | –l |\n| Inva1id/b1ank votes |  | – | – | – |\n| Total | 583,388 | lO0 | 100 | 0 |\n| Registered voters/turnout | 752,692 |  | – | – |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/202-csv/191/nt-1905/191_nt-1905_ocr_char_misinterpret.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "ocr_char_misinterpret", "reasoning": "The table is riddled with OCR character substitutions — for instance, 'l64,376' should be '164,376', 'lO.B' should be '10.8', 'Z5' should be '25', and '76,60S' should be '76,605'. Looking at the row for 'Inva1id/b1ank votes' (itself an OCR-garbled form of 'Invalid/blank votes'), the Votes cell is empty, meaning no votes were recorded for that category. Since the Total row shows 583,388 votes and the individual party votes sum to the same total, the number of invalid or blank votes is 0.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Pervasive OCR substitutions across the table: 'l' for '1' (e.g., l64,376, ll.Z, lO.B), 'O' for '0', 'Z' for '2', 'B' for '8', 'S' for '5' (76,60S), and '0ther' for 'Other'", "answer": 0, "dtype": "int"}
