Angul's population) and commas as decimal separators (e.g., '11,55' for growth percentage). Reading the Child Population column with this convention, Koraput shows 215.518 meaning 215,518 and Puri shows 164.388 meaning 164,388. The difference is 215,518 − 164,388 = 51,130.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Population values like '1.271.703' use dots as thousands separators and commas as decimal separators (e.g., '11,55' for percentage), indicating a European-style decimal_separator_swap throughout the table.", "answer": "51,130", "dtype": "string"}
{"index": "nt-1486", "query": "on average how many of the swara have a short name that begin with d or g?", "table_markdown": "| Position | Swara | Short name | Notation | Mnemonic |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Shadja | Sa | S | sa |\n| 2 | Shuddha Rishabha | Ri | R1 | ra |\n| 3 | Chatushruti Rishabha | Ri | R2 | ri |\n| 3 | Shuddha Gandhara | Ga | G1 | ga |\n| 4 | Shatshruti Rishabha | Ri | R3 | ru |\n| 4 | Sadharana Gandhara | Ga | G2 | gi |\n| 5 | Antara Gandhara | Ga | G3 | gu |\n| 6 | Shuddha Madhyama | Ma | M1 | ma |\n| 7 | Prati Madhyama | Ma | M2 | mi |\n| 8 | Panchama | Pa | P | pa |\n| 9 | Shuddha Dhaivata | Dha | D1 | dha |\n| 10 | Chatushruti Dhaivata | Dha | D2 | dhi |\n| 10 | Shuddha Nishada | Ni | N1 | na |\n| 11 | Shatshruti Dhaivata | Dha | D3 | dhu |\n| 11 | Kaisiki Nishada | Ni | N2 | ni |\n| 12 | Kakali Nishada | Ni | N3 | nu |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Inputs/Dataset/203-csv/352.xlsx", "is_distorted": false, "distortion_type": null, "diversification_type": "original", "reasoning": "Looking at the Short name column, I need to count entries that begin with 'D' or 'G'. The rows with Short name starting with 'G' are: G1 (Shuddha Gandhara), G2 (Sadharana Gandhara), and G3 (Antara Gandhara) — that's 3. The rows with Short name starting with 'D' are: D1 (Shuddha Dhaivata), D2 (Chatushruti Dhaivata), and D3 (Shatshruti Dhaivata) — another 3. Adding these together gives 3 + 3 = 6.", "detection_point": "none", "anomaly_evidence": "", "answer": 6, "dtype": "int"}
{"index": "nt-1510_ocr_char_misinterpret", "query": "which child was the first to die?", "table_markdown": "| Image | Name | Birth date | Death date | Brief biography |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | Harriet Churchil1 | 0ctober l679 | 0ctober l679 | Died an infant |\n|  | Henrietta Churchi11 (later Godolphin), 2nd Duchess of Mar1borough in her own right | 19 July 1681 | 24 October 1733 | Married The Hon. Francis Godolphin (later Viscount Ria1ton by courtesy and Znd Earl of Godo1phin respectively) on 23 April l698. Their son, Wil1iam, Marquess of 81andford died in 1731; their daughter Henrietta married Thomas Pelham-Ho1les, 1st Duke of Newcastle-upon-Tyne; their daughter Mary married Thomas Osborne, 4th Duke of Leeds. |\n|  | Anne Churchil1 (1ater Spencer) | 27 February 1683 | l5 Apri1 1716 | Anne married the Whig politician Char1es Spencer, 3rd Earl of Sunder1and on 14 5eptember 1699. After her sister Henrietta's death in 1733, their son, Charles became 3rd Duke of Marlborough. Their son John is the ancestor of the Ear1s Spencer and thus of Diana, Princess of Wa1es. Their daughter Diana nearly married Frederick, Prince of Wales, but when the p1an failed, married Lord John Russel1, later 4th Duke of Bedford. |\n|  | John Churchi11, Marquess of Blandford | 13 February 1686 | 20 February 1703 | John, Marquess of Blandford was the heir to the Dukedom of Mar1borough. He died unmarried and without children in l703, so the dukedom passed to Marlborough's eldest daughter, Henrietta, after his death in 17O3. |\n|  | E1izabeth Churchill (1ater Egerton) | 15 March l6B7 | 2Z March l738 | Married Scroop Egerton, 4th Earl of 8ridgewater (from 17Z0, 1st Duke of 8ridgewater) on 9 February 1703, 1eaving issue |\n|  | Mary Churchill (later Montagu) | lS Ju1y l6B9 | l4 May l729 | Married John Montagu, 2nd Duke of Montagu on 17 March l705, 1eaving issue |\n| — | Char1es Churchill | 19 August 1690 | 22 May 1692 | Died an infant |", "data_file": "dev_test/Distortions/Output/05082026/204-csv/626/nt-1510/626_nt-1510_ocr_char_misinterpret.xlsx", "is_distorted": true, "distortion_type": "semantic", "diversification_type": "ocr_char_misinterpret", "reasoning": "The table is riddled with OCR character substitutions — names read 'Churchil1' instead of 'Churchill', dates show '0ctober l679' instead of 'October 1679', and digits are swapped throughout (e.g., 'l6B7' for '1687', '2Z March' for '22 March'). To find the first child to die, I need to compare death dates: repairing the OCR, Harriet Churchill died in October 1679, Charles Churchill died 22 May 1692, John Churchill died 20 February 1703, and the others died later still. The earliest death date is October 1679, so the first child to die was Harriet Churchill.", "detection_point": "upfront", "anomaly_evidence": "Pervasive OCR substitutions across nearly every cell: '1' for 'l' in names like 'Churchil1', '0' for 'O' in '0ctober', 'B' for '8' in 'l6B7'/'l6B9', 'Z' for '2' in '2Z March', 'S' for '5' in 'lS Ju1y', etc.", "answer": "Harriet Churchill", "dtype": "string"}
